01. S3 Transfer Acceleration:
If users around the world upload large files to an S3 bucket, S3 Transfer Acceleration can use AWS edge locations to accelerate transfers over long distances.

Conceptually:

User
 |
 v
AWS Edge Location
 |
 v
S3 Bucket

Useful for globally distributed uploaders.



02. S3 Presigned URL:
Very important real-world concept.
Suppose your S3 bucket is private.

You don't want:

Internet → Public S3 bucket

Instead:

Application
    |
    | Generate presigned URL
    v
Temporary URL
    |
    v
User
    |
    v
Private S3 Object

A presigned URL grants time-limited access to a specific S3 object.

Example:

User wants:
invoice123.pdf

Application
     ↓
Generate presigned URL
     ↓
User downloads file

The bucket can remain private.




03. Multipart Upload:
For large files, S3 supports multipart uploads.

Instead of:

10 GB file
    |
    v
Upload everything at once

you can conceptually do:

10 GB
 |
 +-- Part 1
 +-- Part 2
 +-- Part 3
 +-- Part 4
 +-- ...
 |
 v
S3
 |
 v
Complete multipart upload

Advantages include:

Better throughput
Parallel uploads
Ability to retry individual parts.



04. S3 CLI:
Very important for DevOps.

Configure AWS CLI:

aws configure

List buckets:

aws s3 ls

Create bucket:

aws s3 mb s3://my-demo-bucket

Upload:

aws s3 cp app.zip s3://my-demo-bucket/

Download:

aws s3 cp s3://my-demo-bucket/app.zip .

Copy directory:

aws s3 cp ./build s3://my-demo-bucket/build/ --recursive

Sync:

aws s3 sync ./build s3://my-demo-bucket/build/




05. cp vs sync
Very common DevOps question.

cp

Copies specified files/objects.

aws s3 cp app.zip s3://bucket/
sync

Synchronizes directories.

aws s3 sync ./build s3://bucket/build/

Example:

Local:
build/
├── index.html
├── app.js
└── style.css

Run:
aws s3 sync build/ s3://my-site/



